<b>ד.</b> Parashat Akeda—to recall ancestral merit daily and subdue one’s yetzer like Yitzchak who gave his soul; parashat haman—so one believes all sustenance comes through Divine providence; parashat korbanot—for end of Menachot says “this is the law of the chatat”—whoever engages in Torah of chatat is as if he offered a chatat…<br>
<br>
<b>ה.</b> “Specifically as an individual…”—originally they read [the Ten Commandments] publicly as Tamid chapter states; Berakhot end—they abolished them because of sectarians who claimed the rest of the Torah untrue—that concern applies in public; alone there is no such grievance.
